<!--
    Every word the app says, in one place.

    Capitalisation follows Microsoft's "Text guidelines – capitalization" (UI Design and Interaction
    Guide for Windows Phone 7, v2.0), which the phone itself followed:

      lowercase       page titles, list titles, list group titles, buttons and commands (app-bar
                      captions, menu items, context menus), list items, search box hints
      Sentence caps   explanatory text, status and notification text, progress text ("Scanning…"),
                      toggle switches (SettingRow raises the first letter of its header itself, so
                      those strings stay lowercase here)
      ALL CAPS        application titles (the overlines)

    Proper nouns keep their capitals inside sentence-caps text (Android, Last.fm, Genius, USB). The
    same guide's punctuation rules apply too: no ellipsis on a command ("merge into", not "merge
    into…"), an ellipsis on progress text, and a closing full stop only on instructional text. A
    translation should keep all of this wherever the target language's orthography allows.

    Adding a language means one folder — values-ru/strings.xml and so on — and nothing else; there is
    no user-visible literal left in the Kotlin. Counts go through <plurals> rather than through
    "%d songs", because "2 песни" and "5 песен" are different forms and a format string cannot know
    that.
-->
