    <string name="playback_loudness">loudness</string>
    <string name="playback_sleep">sleep</string>
    <string name="playback_resume">resuming</string>
    <string name="resume_position">remember where I stopped in tracks longer than</string>
    <string name="resume_position_off">never — every track starts from the top</string>
    <!-- The threshold in the resume row, e.g. "15 minutes". -->
    <string name="resume_position_value">%1$d minutes</string>
    <string name="resume_position_explainer">A song comes back from its beginning, which is what a
        song is for. A podcast or an audiobook two hours long does not: past this length the app
        keeps the exact moment you were at, and the next launch picks up from there.</string>
    <string name="playback_normalize">even out the volume between tracks</string>
    <string name="playback_normalize_explainer">Uses the ReplayGain tags your files were given when
        they were ripped or tagged. It can only turn a loud track down, never a quiet one up, and a
        file with no such tag plays as it is.</string>
    <string name="library_album_artist">believe the album artist tag</string>
    <string name="library_album_artist_explainer">A record is filed under its album artist rather
        than under whoever its first track credits — which is what keeps a compilation together, and
        an album whose every track says “band feat. someone” under the band. Turn it off if a ripper
        wrote something odd in that tag. Needs Android 11.</string>
    <string name="library_artists_album_artist">artists from the album artist</string>
    <string name="library_artists_album_artist_explainer">Every track goes to one artist — the first
        name its album artist tag gives — so a record tagged “band, orchestra, guest, guest” belongs to
        the band and the guests are not artists of their own. The first name only, because plenty of
        files repeat the whole guest list in that tag as well. Leave splitting on above, or there is no
        first name to take. A track with no album artist tag goes under the first name of its own
        credit. Turns believing that tag on with it.</string>
    <string name="library_prefer_known_artist">give a shared track to the artist you have most of</string>
    <string name="library_prefer_known_artist_explainer">When a tag names several artists, the track
        goes to whichever of them has the most records here instead of to whoever is written first —
        so “craspore; angel vox” lands under angel vox if that is whose music you have. A guess on top
        of the tags rather than what they say, which is why it is separate. Turns the two settings
        above on with it.</string>
    <string name="library_fix_artists">fix artists doubling</string>
    <string name="library_fix_artists_explainer">Treats “Blink 182”, “Blink-182” and “Blink‐182” as
        one artist, keeping the spelling most of your files use. A space, a hyphen and a dash between
        the same words are a difference in the tags, not in the band.</string>
